Settings.Device.KeepAlive.Hint=Some sound outputs — HDMI especially — power down the moment the sound stops, and then take a moment to wake up again. What you hear is the first word of a sentence going missing, over and over, with nothing obviously wrong. While this is on, NBR plays a tone far below anything you can hear on the card it is using, so the card never gets the chance to fall asleep between sentences. Turn it off if your card does not do this: it holds the output open for as long as a book is playing.
Settings.Device.Default=System default (auto)
; The optical-drive group. The tab is "Devices" because a sound card and a CD
; drive are two different pieces of hardware; it was named for having only one.
Settings.Device.OpticalGroup=Optical drive
Settings.Device.UseOptical=Use the optical drive for audio CDs
Settings.Device.OpticalWhich=Drive:
Settings.Device.OpticalAuto=Whichever has a disc in it
Settings.Device.UseOptical.Hint=Books were once published on ordinary audio CDs, and NBR can play them. It reads the disc itself rather than leaving it spinning: the whole side is read in one pass, straight through and without going back, so the drive works for a few minutes and is then silent for the rest of the book. Reading slowly would be quieter for a second and noisier for an hour. Turn this on only if you want it - it is off to begin with, even on a machine that has a drive, and while it is off NBR never touches the drive at all.
