Dict.Help=Regex help...
Dict.Help.Title=Regular expressions in the pronunciation dictionary
Dict.Help.Text=The old Zen wisdom says:\n\n    Developer had a problem.\n    Developer decided to solve the problem with regular expressions.\n    Developer had two problems.\n\nRegEx, or regular expressions, are something you would rather not deal with, but it is fancy to have that option, so here it is.\n\nIf you know what you are doing, knock yourself out. If not, just don't use it, or ask Google.
Settings.Misc.Look=Look:
Settings.Misc.Look.Follow=Follow Windows
Settings.Misc.Look.Classic=System colours
Settings.Misc.Look.New=NBR design
Settings.Misc.Look.Hint=How NBR draws itself.\n\nFollow Windows is the usual answer: NBR uses its own design, except when you have a high contrast theme switched on in Windows, and then it steps aside and uses your colours instead.\n\nSystem colours makes it do that always — plain windows in whatever colours and fonts Windows is set to, which is also what works best with a magnifier.\n\nNBR design makes it use its own look always, high contrast or not. That is your call to make: if it turns out not to suit you, the keyboard and your screen reader are unaffected, and F2 brings you back here.
Settings.Misc.Look.RestartTitle=Change the look
Settings.Misc.Look.Restart=The look changes when NBR starts again. Restart it now? Your place in the book is saved either way.

; Per-control help: a ? on each group opens one short explanation. F1 on the
; focused control opens the same text without travelling to the button.
Hint.Button.Accessible=Help for {0}
Hint.SoundProcessing=Using sound processing you can fine-tune the sound of the recording. You can remove different noises, boost the volume and colour the overall sound with the equaliser. There are six groups of controls, each of which you can turn on and off separately. You can also tune each control separately. You can temporarily bypass the whole sound processing chain by pressing Ctrl+B, to hear the difference between the original recording and your sound settings.
Hint.RemoveRumble=Cuts the very lowest frequencies — traffic, air conditioning, handling noise. It cannot recover a muddy recording, but it clears the bottom end so speech has room.
Hint.NoiseRemoval=Reduces steady background hiss, the kind a tape or a cheap microphone leaves behind. Too strong a setting starts to eat the voice with it, so raise it only until the hiss goes.
Hint.SoftenSibilance=Takes the edge off sharp S and Š sounds. Useful on close-miked readers and on recordings that were brightened too much.
Hint.EvenOutSpeech=Narrows the gap between the quiet and the loud parts, so a whisper and a shout end up closer together. This is the one to reach for when you keep adjusting the volume.
Hint.Tone=Five bands, in decibels, named by their frequency. The lowest sits at 200 Hz, which is where a boomy or muddy recording usually is; the top one is a shelf, so it lifts or lowers everything above it rather than one region. The bands overlap on purpose, which makes a smooth curve possible but also means they add up -- three neighbouring bands at +4 give rather more than +4 between them. Small moves go a long way, and anything past a few dB usually means another stage is the real fix.
Hint.AutomaticLoudness=Two things that pull against each other, which is why they share a box. Automatic loudness holds the overall level steady across a whole book, including between files recorded on different days; it follows the voice rather than the whole signal, which is what a spoken recording asks for. Noise in pauses works the other way: anything quieter than the chosen level gets pushed down, so a hiss or a hum that sits under the reading stops being audible in the gaps between sentences. It never touches the voice itself, and it quietens rather than silences (a noise that switches off and on around every phrase is more tiring than one that simply stays). Turn loudness up and the noise underneath comes up with it, so if that happens, this is the control for it.
Hint.Text0=Here you can choose the language and voice for reading this book. You can have different settings for each individual book in the Library. Every voice remembers its own settings — speed, volume and pitch — so there is no need to set them again after switching back from another voice.\n\nRemember that you can select "No speech" if you want to read visually or on the braille display without hearing audio.
Hint.TextBrailleSource=This book came from a braille file, and this is the table it was read with — the one that turned the braille cells back into words.\n\nA braille file says nothing about its language, its grade, or which national standard it follows, so NBR has to work the table out from the text itself. It is usually right, but not always, and a wrong table gives words that are almost right: "Have" where the book says "How".\n\nIf that is what you are seeing, pick another table here and press OK. The book is read again from the braille file you imported, which is kept beside it, so you can try as many tables as you like and go back to the first one at any time.\n\nThe tables offered are the ones for the language above. If that language is wrong, change it and the tables will follow.\n\nThis has nothing to do with what your braille display shows. Your screen reader translates that, using the table in its own settings.
Hint.TextVisual=Here you can adjust the screen layout for reading. You can choose between a subtitle-like layout, scrolling text like film end credits, or instant page changes. You can also choose the background, text and highlight colours, and whether the line being read or the whole sentence is marked.\n\nNote that this changes nothing about the speech.

